00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:51.440
So firstly, when we think
about unstructured data,

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:52.910
every single
company in the world

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:55.700
has an advertising arm
and a marketing arm.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.750
And we realize that we're
sitting on a lot of images

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:01.880
that we could then
use to help generate

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:03.620
these valuable
business insights.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:05.960
So I'll start with a
very basic definition

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.360
of what is a creative because
I understand we may not all

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.830
have a marketing background.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.290
So creatives are
the visual materials

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.260
that are used to generate
leads and sell advertising

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:16.138
or marketing.

00:07:16.138 --> 00:07:17.430
We are all familiar with these.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:18.930
We're here today
at Next, and I know

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.740
I've seen a bunch of
Google Cloud creatives

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.040
as I've been walking through.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.100
But I just wanted to
level set that this is

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.260
what we're talking about today.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:28.910
And more specifically,
we're talking

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:30.630
about creative analysis.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:34.580
So that's the comparison between
two or more of these images

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:36.270
to determine changes
in performance.

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:38.520
So when we're thinking about
a marketing organization,

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.760
we think about things
like click through rate,

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.860
impressions, clicks as
metrics of performance.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:48.080
And we want to understand
how these two images compare

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:50.825
and contrast in performance,
as they're both here showcasing

00:07:50.825 --> 00:07:54.110
the same lip balm.

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:56.220
So here we have the question.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:59.600
Do lifestyle creatives perform
better than product creatives?

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.250
An example of a
product creative is

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.990
this one here in
the middle, where

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:04.580
we're showing just the product.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:06.530
And a lifestyle
example is the one

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:08.270
here on the right,
where we actually

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:11.880
have a model who's holding and
interacting with the product.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.050
Now, as humans, this is
really easy for us to look at.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.300
We can look and we can
see that on the right

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.700
there's a model, on the
left there isn't, therefore

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:22.280
we very easily
classify this problem.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.540
But to Sjef's point earlier, how
can we have a machine do this

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:27.930
at scale?

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:30.410
And that's what the power
of Google Machine Learning

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.539
can really bring to
the picture here.

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:36.798
It'll help us to be able
to identify these two.

00:08:36.798 --> 00:08:38.840
The other question you
might be asking yourselves

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:40.940
is why should we
focus on creatives?

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:44.810
There are so many other aspects
of an advertising or marketing

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:48.740
campaign, like
targeting, like recency,

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:50.900
like reach for example,
that we've been hyper

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:53.340
focused on as an industry.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.820
However, according to
a recent Nielsen study,

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:00.500
we saw that creatives still
contribute to about 45%

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:01.970
of overall sales.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:04.700
So making sure that we
have high creative quality

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.350
is imperative to our business.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.270
And it's almost
a no brainer when

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:09.770
we're running these
ads to make sure

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:12.830
that the content of that
creative is of high quality

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:17.030
and resonating with our
users and our customers.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.880
So that being said,
if it's a no brainer

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:21.840
and we feel like everybody
should be doing it

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:25.580
and 50% of the business
relies on it, why aren't we?

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:28.790
So the main challenges that
we face today are threefold.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:32.180
It's expensive, it's
manual, and it's rigid.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:34.100
So it's expensive
and manual because it

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.110
takes a lot of human effort.

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.140
If we think about our two
creatives that we saw earlier,

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.180
today we would need
humans to actually go

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.400
in and tag that the
creative on the right

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:44.630
was a lifestyle creative.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:47.130
It had a model in it, et cetera.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:49.790
And so that really
limits us to only looking

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.830
at a subset of our creatives.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:54.220
The other thing is that
it's a very rigid process

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:58.340
in that we have to think half of
these use cases ahead of time.

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:00.060
Because it's so
expensive and so manual,

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:02.060
we want to make sure that
we're getting the most

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:05.550
bang for our buck as we're going
through this analysis process.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:07.670
So with Google and
Jellyfish, we thought

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:10.430
that there has to be
a better way of doing

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:13.280
this creative analysis piece,
and we used Google Cloud

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.120
technology to help do that.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.270
So the first thing is
that we reduced cost

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:18.680
using the Vision API.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:20.900
And I'll go through each
of these pieces in detail,

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.355
but I just wanted to
generally introduce them.

00:10:23.355 --> 00:10:24.980
So we reduced costs
by using the Vision

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:29.630
API, which Sjef introduced, to
do automatic creative analysis.

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:33.590
Then we helped to automate that
analysis by implementing a data

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.620
pipeline so that way we're not
just looking at one creative.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:39.260
We can look at
multiple creatives.

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:40.940
And then lastly,
we used App Engine

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:42.950
to allow for flexible
scaling here.

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:45.200
So now instead of just
looking at a set of 100,

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:49.250
we could scale up to 1,000 or
multi thousands or even more

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:51.090
using the same process.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.670
So that's how we
solved for this.

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:56.690
So the first thing that we did
was evaluating the Cloud Vision

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:57.590
APIs.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:00.500
While it might be fun to upload
pictures of fried chicken

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:03.110
and labradoodles, does
this actually apply

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:05.260
to advertising
creatives, and does it

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:09.070
have any business value for
our marketing organizations?

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.320
So that was the first thing
that we wanted to try.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.960
And here, if we look
at the sample creative,

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:16.570
we had uploaded this
to the Vision API

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:19.000
and we saw that it
pulled out three labels

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.520
that we thought were
really important in doing

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:23.060
this kind of analysis.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.980
The first is that it was able to
identify very accurately faces

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:27.830
and emotions.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:29.590
So not only can we
now see that there's

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:31.690
a model in those creatives,
but we could actually

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:34.330
see the emotions that they're
expressing, whether it's

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:37.630
joy, sorrow, anger, et cetera.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.220
The next thing was that we
could do textual analysis.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.310
So in the bottom right,
where it says learn more,

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:45.070
it was able to identify
the text piece.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.620
And then, lastly, we
were able to identify

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:48.950
the dominant colors.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:51.820
So this helps to ensure
the quality of the creative

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:54.070
by ensuring that our
creatives are on brand

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:57.190
and using the color scheme
that's been recommended.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.020
So that first part
was a success,

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:02.230
and we were able to prove that
it worked for one creative.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:04.480
But I don't know about you
guys, for me I don't really

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.720
appreciate uploading
one creative at a time

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:08.950
and downloading them.

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:11.500
So to that end, we
went ahead and created

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:15.940
a data pipeline that took our
creatives out of campaign.

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:19.270
manager, which is a Google
Marketing platform product that

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:22.240
houses all of an advertiser's
campaign information

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:24.620
and creative information
in one place.

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:26.830
So this was the data
source for those creatives.

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:29.500
We then took them
and used App Engine

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:31.360
for our pipeline orchestration.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:34.990
So this was where it kicked
off the flow for the rest

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:36.640
of the data pipeline.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.400
And what it did is
that it published jobs

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:42.550
to Cloud Pub/Sub, which is
an asynchronous messaging

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:45.590
queue, that then kicked off
two separate work streams.

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:48.890
The first is storing these
raw assets in cloud storage

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:51.310
so we could do analysis
on them, and the second

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:53.410
was running them
through the Vision API

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.450
to do image analysis,
and then storing

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:58.600
all of those results
back into BigQuery

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:00.820
to run our results off of.

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:04.510
So this is the architecture,
and we'll actually

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:06.380
go through a live demo of this.

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:09.010
So let's see this in reality.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.960
If we're looking at
this example here,

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:13.210
one of the first
customers that Jellyfish

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.970
went to market with
for this project

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:17.770
was with Walden University.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:20.950
So here we have an example of
two creatives, creative number

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:22.930
one and creative
number two, and we

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:26.490
want to understand which
creatives show happy faces.

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:28.240
And if you guys have
been to the keynotes

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:31.090
like many of our leaders have
said, seeing is believing,

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:36.010
so let's jump in to a live
demo of how this process works

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:37.930
and we'll switch
over to the demo now.

00:13:42.802 --> 00:13:44.260
Could we please
switch to the demo?

00:13:44.260 --> 00:13:45.288
Great.

00:13:45.288 --> 00:13:46.830
So the very first
thing that we'll do

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.690
is we'll actually try
out the Vision API.

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:51.900
So here, this is
an online website.

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.630
Anybody can go to
cloud.google.com/vision

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.670
and upload it with
your own creatives,

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:58.650
so I would recommend
that you do that.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:00.810
Here I have downloaded
the creatives already,

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:03.300
so I'm just going to go
ahead and drag and drop.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:06.600
Verify that I am
not a robot in fact.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.305
And we'll see the analysis here.

00:14:09.305 --> 00:14:10.680
So in a matter of
seconds we were

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:13.050
able to get back the API calls.

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:15.660
Like I mentioned earlier,
we wanted to look at faces,

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:17.130
so here we've
correctly identified

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:18.900
that there is a
face in the picture

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:20.730
and that joy is very likely.

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:24.000
So automatically, we're now
identifying the creatives that

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.760
have happy faces versus
the ones that don't.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:28.260
The other thing
that I had mentioned

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:29.650
was the text analysis.

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:31.530
So here we're able
to pull out the text

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:33.900
from each and every
single creative

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:36.580
and understand where they are.

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:39.780
So let's go ahead and
reset this and try it

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.830
with our second creative.

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:44.280
So let's make sure it
works on both creatives

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.930
before we move forward
with a data pipeline piece.

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:50.520
And again, we're able to see
that the faces were identified

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.750
and that this face
is very neutral.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.730
It's not showing any
likelihood of any emotion.

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:57.480
The other thing that
I wanted to highlight

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:59.280
is that this, of
course, is a UI view

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:01.560
so it's a very small view
of what we'll actually

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:04.110
be able to see when
we call the API.

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:05.760
Here if we look
at the JSON we can

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:09.000
see that these are all
the information that's

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:10.830
being recorded as the response.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:14.790
So we can actually see, for
example, the bounding polygon

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:16.780
and the vertices
of the face itself.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:19.320
So it really enables
you to do whatever kinds

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:23.650
of creative insights it is that
you want to as an organization.

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:26.650
So now that we've proven that
the Vision API piece works,

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:29.940
let's go ahead and go into
the actual data pipeline.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.010
We're going to go
into Campaign Manager,

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:37.170
like I mentioned earlier, which
is the ad server tool that's

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:38.730
a part of Google
Marketing platform.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:41.520
Here we're in Walton
University's creatives,

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:44.340
and we can actually
see the number

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.930
of creatives that have ran.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:49.530
And I can scroll down
for eons on this.

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:51.840
And if I just zoom into
one of these creatives,

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:54.570
we can see some of the issues.

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:56.460
The creative names
here are really

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.570
made for campaign performance.

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:01.260
It helps us identify which
campaign this creative was

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.000
a part of, but it
really doesn't give us

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.580
any information about the
content of the creative itself.

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:07.920
So what we'll do is--

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:10.920
right before I got on stage
I kicked of the data pipeline

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:14.700
already, so we'll watch the
data pipeline as it progresses.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.130
And what it's doing is it's
taking this list of creatives

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:18.690
and putting it
through that process

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:20.280
that I had mentioned earlier.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:24.960
So I'll go ahead and go into
my Google Cloud Platform,

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.600
and here we can see
that I've kicked off

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:29.460
the job in App Engine.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:32.640
We can also follow the
logs in Stackdriver.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.830
So here, if I zoom
into our logs,

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:38.200
we can see that it's split
out into two sections.

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:40.590
The first is the
visionary, so that's

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:42.600
the Vision API actually
doing the analysis

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.310
on each individual creative.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:48.120
And then the second piece
here is the gcs_uploader.

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:51.930
So again, that is how we're
uploading this information back

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:53.730
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.800
And then lastly, I
wanted to show the data

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.240
once it lands in BigQuery.

00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:00.960
Because while I really enjoy
seeing all the details of how

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.180
this pipeline works, I'm
sure that for the business

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:04.555
folks in the room
you really want

00:17:04.555 --> 00:17:07.900
to see what is the value
of this data once it lands.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:11.250
So here I've gone ahead and
pulled together a query.

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:13.800
And before I go into it, I
just wanted to highlight here

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:15.069
on the left hand side.

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:18.660
We have the various
tables that came out

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.319
as a result of
our data pipeline.

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:22.170
So we have the face detection.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:23.900
We have additional
image properties.

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:26.670
We have label detection,
logos, et cetera.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:28.230
And then lastly,
we're able to join

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:29.920
this with performance data.

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.640
So now we're able to really
implement creative analysis

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:36.820
by seeing that performance data
side by side with our images.

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:39.390
So if I go ahead
and run this query,

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:42.960
here what we're going
to see is the results.

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.960
So these are the two creatives
that we looked at earlier,

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:47.970
and we see that in
the first creative

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:52.080
the joy likelihood is
five, which is very high.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:54.180
And the second creative
the joy likelihood is one.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:59.070
So here we have our very happy
face and are not happy face.

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.050
We were asked to redact
the number of clicks

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:02.868
and impressions due
to privacy reasons.

00:18:02.868 --> 00:18:05.160
However, here you would be
able to see that performance

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:08.310
information, and we'd be
able to compare and contrast

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:09.720
the performance itself.

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.120
Jellyfish will actually
go into the details

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.370
of how they were able to
accomplish this test and more

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:15.270
of the value.

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:16.770
But I just wanted
to show how, if we

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:18.390
lay that foundation
of data you're

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:20.585
able to see all of
this in one place.

00:18:20.585 --> 00:18:22.710
The other thing to note
here on the right hand side

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.085
is that we've also been able
to link out the Google Cloud

00:18:25.085 --> 00:18:27.300
Storage URL, so
if anybody wanted

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:29.920
to go through and do a manual
check of these creatives

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.760
it's really easy to do that.

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:37.200
Before we leave our demo,
the last thing I wanted to do

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:40.780
was uncomment this
one line in our query.

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:42.680
So what if we had
decided instead

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:46.160
of looking at happy faces I
want to understand if people who

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:49.160
are wearing graduation
caps in my creatives

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.890
were able to perform
better or worse?

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:56.780
If I uncomment out this face
annotation headwear likelihood

00:18:56.780 --> 00:19:00.500
and rerun the query, I'm
able to edit my hypothesis

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:03.670
in just a matter of seconds
and see the results here.

00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:05.420
So here, if you remember
in our creatives,

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:08.120
the headwear-- neither of them
were wearing graduation caps

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:09.923
so we're able to see that here.

00:19:09.923 --> 00:19:11.840
But if you imagine that
we're running this off

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:14.750
of hundreds of creatives,
not just one or two, then

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:17.120
you can see exactly
how impactful

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.090
being able to pull this
data pipeline together is.

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:24.680
So do you mind please now
going back to our presentation?

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:25.180
Great.

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:26.760
So thank you so
much for listening

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:29.670
to the explanation of the Google
tools that help to power this.

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:32.430
I will now hand it over
to James from Jellyfish

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:34.590
to go into the business
value that this tool was

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:35.280
able to provide.

00:19:44.947 --> 00:19:46.280
JAMES RAPPAZZO: My name's James.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:50.022
I'm a data scientist
at Jellyfish.

00:19:50.022 --> 00:19:51.980
For those of you who
don't know, Jellyfish-- we

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:55.880
are a global digital partner,
as well as a Google Marketing

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:59.330
Platform and Google Cloud
Platform certified partner.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:01.010
We offer a wide
variety of services

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:03.590
from creative
development and analysis

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:08.450
to media strategy, data analysis
training, and much more.

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:10.130
As part of the
data team, we build

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:13.220
solutions that help our clients
understand their big data

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:15.560
and then take these insights
to inform their business

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:17.970
processes.

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:19.790
So how are we using
this solution?

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:22.220
Well, it augments our
comprehensive data strategy

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:26.460
by informing our data driven
creative development strategy.

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:28.340
It helps us increase
performance.

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:29.960
As Sona mentioned,
creatives account

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:32.972
for a really significant
amount of sales.

00:20:32.972 --> 00:20:35.180
And if we can just be a
little bit better at creative

00:20:35.180 --> 00:20:38.180
we can deliver significant
performance lift.

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:40.520
And to that end, it helps
us quickly and efficiently

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:42.140
discover what works
and what doesn't

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:44.893
with creative development.

00:20:44.893 --> 00:20:46.310
So the first thing
we wanted to do

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:49.970
was see if there was any
meaningful information

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:52.980
that we could pull out
from the Vision API.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.280
And so we set up
a series of tests

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:58.070
using the adjusted
viewable click through rate

00:20:58.070 --> 00:20:59.980
as our performance
metric, which is

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:03.110
essentially clicks over
total viewable impressions.

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:05.060
We then split our
creatives into two groups--

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:07.730
having and not having
a specific feature.

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:09.560
And then finally, used
a two sample t-test

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:12.670
to analyze significance.

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:15.070
So our first hypothesis
was that creatives

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:17.440
with happy facial
expressions have historically

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:20.920
performed better than creatives
with faces that did not

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:23.210
have a happy expression.

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:27.260
There were 184 creatives with
happy faces and 16 without,

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:29.210
and we found that
creatives with happy faces

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:31.160
performed two times
better than creatives

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:34.433
without happy
facial expressions.

00:21:34.433 --> 00:21:35.850
For our second
hypothesis, we were

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:38.392
really interested in seeing what
makes call to action buttons

00:21:38.392 --> 00:21:39.925
effective.

00:21:39.925 --> 00:21:41.300
In order to do
that, we had to do

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:42.510
a bit of feature
engineering, which

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:45.010
is one of the really cool things
with the Google partnership

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:47.580
is that we're able to
build on each other's work.

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:50.520
So I used OpenCV, the
computer vision library,

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:54.210
to extract the location,
color, contrast, size,

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:56.020
et cetera of the buttons.

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:58.020
And then once we had the
location of the button,

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:01.950
we could cross-reference
that with the Vision API data

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:04.752
to find the text that
appears inside the button.

00:22:04.752 --> 00:22:06.210
And then from there
we can find out

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:10.740
what impacts performance,
positively or negatively,

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:13.230
in terms of the text.

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:17.100
So our second hypothesis is that
creatives with other text aside

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:19.020
from "Follow your
Why" in the CTA button

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.330
have higher historical
performance than creatives

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:24.360
with "Follow your Why"
in the CTA button.

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:27.120
And there were 172 creatives
with other text, 16

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:28.620
with "Follow your
Why," and we found

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:32.323
that creatives with other
text have historically

00:22:32.323 --> 00:22:33.990
performed two times
better than creators

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:35.973
with "Follow your Why."

00:22:35.973 --> 00:22:37.640
So we're really excited
about this tool.

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:41.220
It enables us to do much
deeper creative analysis,

00:22:41.220 --> 00:22:43.350
and we've started working
on a Self-Serve Analytics

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:46.350
Dashboard, which will basically
allow our clients to track

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:49.380
the general performance
of the different features

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:50.832
of their creatives.

00:22:50.832 --> 00:22:52.290
And then we'll use
this information

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:55.320
to inform our
hypothesis-driven analysis

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:56.490
on the historical data.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:59.160
And then, finally, we're going
to start exploring and building

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:01.170
a machine learning model
to predict performance

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.557
based on the feature mix.

00:23:03.557 --> 00:23:05.890
Thank you so much, and I'm
going to give it back to Sjef

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:07.900
to talk about marketing
insights from text.

00:23:10.365 --> 00:23:11.740
SJEF VAN STIPHOUT:
Thanks, James.

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:12.390
It was awesome.

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:14.182
So I realized I forgot
to introduce myself,

00:23:14.182 --> 00:23:15.563
but I got a second chance.

00:23:15.563 --> 00:23:16.230
My name is Sjef.

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:18.580
I'm a Marketing Technology
Specialist in Google Cloud.

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:21.280
It means that I'm
basically a custom engineer

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:24.340
but I work with our
customers when they either

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:25.870
bring in their own
advertising data

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:29.230
or they have their on
premise marketing solution,

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:32.590
how to leverage Google Cloud
and the advertising side

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:35.380
of the house in our products.

00:23:35.380 --> 00:23:37.090
I have the privilege
here to be with Tim.

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:39.160
Tim joined us from
Colgate-Palmolive,

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:41.530
and we worked on a project
that I will tell you about,

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:44.230
where we did something
similar as James showed you

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:46.810
and Sona but then with text.

00:23:46.810 --> 00:23:52.705
And it's hard to
underestimate the value of

00:23:52.705 --> 00:23:54.520
and the role of online reviews.

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:57.790
So written text these days--
whatever is out there.

00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:00.820
If it's on product review
sites or it's on Reddit,

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:03.550
if it's on blogs,
consumers are offering up

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:05.050
a lot of information
about what they

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:07.550
think of your products, what
they think of your brands, what

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:10.660
went well, what went wrong.

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:12.250
There's a lot of
reviews out there.

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:15.580
I looked up some numbers,
and I believe, for instance,

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:21.060
Yelp in 2018 had a number of 171
million reviews on their site.

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:23.050
There's no way you're
going to read those.

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:26.860
Maybe you have hired people
to read those reviews

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:32.800
and to pick up on problems or
to think about new features

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:34.720
that you can add to your
product or your hotel

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:36.220
or whatever business you're in.

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:38.038
But that's a lot
of work, and you

00:24:38.038 --> 00:24:40.330
might miss very important
signals, especially if you're

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:45.200
talking about thousands of SKUs
that you would have to monitor.

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:49.790
This is where natural language
processing really has its

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:53.750
benefit, and Google Cloud
has two out-of-the-box ways

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:56.120
to really leverage
that technology.

00:24:56.120 --> 00:24:58.130
One is pre-trained
machine learning models.

00:24:58.130 --> 00:25:01.040
They come in the form of an
API, and that's just an API call

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:05.210
like we saw with Sona and
James with the Vision API.

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:08.430
There was a version of it
that does natural text.

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:10.490
So it just sends the
body of text to the API

00:25:10.490 --> 00:25:16.370
and it will return the fields
with the different dimensions.

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:18.642
You can also train
your custom models.

00:25:18.642 --> 00:25:20.600
This is what I was talking
about earlier, where

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:23.030
you have this hybrid model where
you leverage what Google knows

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:23.960
and what you know.

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:26.630
Especially if you're in
a very specific sector,

00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:29.860
like if you're in law or
it's a medical journal.

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:32.510
It might be jargon
and certain subtleties

00:25:32.510 --> 00:25:36.230
that you as a company really
are aware of but Google didn't

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:38.830
optimize their models for.

00:25:38.830 --> 00:25:41.450
That's really where
Cloud's AutoML comes in.

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:44.720
So you train it to look
for those specificities

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:46.770
that you know that
are out there,

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:49.730
and then we'll see solutions
that start with an API

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:51.560
graduating in AutoML.

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:53.930
As soon as you start to
see where that value is,

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:56.720
that is really your next
step to really leverage

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:59.940
those subtleties.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.280
So the Cloud Natural
Language API.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.230
This is what comes out.

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:08.000
So you've got a general score
of the whole body of text.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.020
That comes with a magnitude.

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:14.610
And I think we often talk
about mag sentiment analysis,

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:16.910
but I think
magnitude, or salience

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:20.300
as we see with entities,
are very important features.

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:22.140
Because something
can be very negative,

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:24.350
but if it's not
central to your text

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:26.180
or if it's not very
pronounced, then you

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:28.100
might want to rank it lower.

00:26:28.100 --> 00:26:30.270
So it's a great way to
start weighing your results.

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:34.460
So even if you use the API
and not an AutoML model,

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:36.910
you're still able to really
pick up on some subtleties

00:26:36.910 --> 00:26:40.190
because you start to see where
the API might overgeneralize

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:41.470
things.

00:26:41.470 --> 00:26:44.240
For entities, this is
where you really leverage

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:45.770
Google's Knowledge Graph.

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:47.500
Google has, for
the last decades,

00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:50.000
really tried to figure out what
are two people talking about

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:51.470
and how can we
identify-- for instance,

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:53.240
in Google search if
you type something in,

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:55.010
what are they
actually referring to?

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:58.033
So there might be five
ways to talk about a car.

00:26:58.033 --> 00:27:00.200
You can now apply that
Knowledge Graph to your text.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:04.220
So we'll say you talked
about the Eiffel Tower.

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:07.250
You talked about a cinema or
you talked about a conference.

00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:09.890
For all those entities,
you get a sentiment score

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:11.540
and a magnitude and
salience, so you

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:13.370
know how important
that element was

00:27:13.370 --> 00:27:16.490
in the total body of the text.

00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:17.740
The same for sentences.

00:27:17.740 --> 00:27:21.477
It will break out the sentences
and it does language detection.

00:27:21.477 --> 00:27:23.060
So if you have an
international market

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:24.477
and you get reviews,
for instance,

00:27:24.477 --> 00:27:29.090
in different languages, then you
can use the API to detect it.

00:27:29.090 --> 00:27:30.565
And it's now available in--

00:27:30.565 --> 00:27:31.940
I'm going to read
it from there--

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:34.790
monitor was English, Spanish,
Japanese, Chinese simplified

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:37.550
and traditional, French, German,
Italian, Korean, Portuguese,

00:27:37.550 --> 00:27:40.460
and Russian, and
this list is growing.

00:27:40.460 --> 00:27:43.062
But you see that, in a
multilingual environment,

00:27:43.062 --> 00:27:45.270
this is really a tool that
also works out of the box,

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:46.160
so you won't have
to translate. you.

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:48.160
Won't have to hire people
in different countries

00:27:48.160 --> 00:27:50.276
to do those analytics.

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:59.080
This is just the first review I
ran into on Colgate toothpaste.

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:02.140
It's this person's
favorite toothpaste.

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:06.010
It's good cavity fighting
toothpaste with added whitener.

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:07.570
The whitening
features really works.

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:09.490
Why wouldn't you
want a bigger smile?

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:11.470
So you see, I'm not going
to be the whole vibe

00:28:11.470 --> 00:28:13.110
because it's actually
a lot of text,

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:14.818
but that really goes
to show that, if you

00:28:14.818 --> 00:28:18.050
want to do this at scale, you
need an automated solution.

00:28:18.050 --> 00:28:21.650
So, similar to what
Sona and James showed,

00:28:21.650 --> 00:28:25.860
we built a pipeline where we can
analyze these reviews at scale.

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:28.700
And it starts with
a storage bucket.

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:32.420
So as you might know, Google,
BigQuery, and other data

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:35.000
analytics really
excel in situations

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:38.240
where you have combinations
of streaming and batching

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:39.120
pipelines.

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:42.620
So this is an example of
how you add batching type

00:28:42.620 --> 00:28:44.820
pipeline to your architecture.

00:28:44.820 --> 00:28:48.550
So let's imagine this review
comes into the storage bucket.

00:28:48.550 --> 00:28:51.060
There is a feature
for Google Storage.

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:53.360
It's called object
notification, and what

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:56.330
it does is, as soon as a file
arrives, or when it's updated,

00:28:56.330 --> 00:29:00.230
it sends a pops up notification
to a topic of your choice.

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:04.190
For people who are not aware
or don't know what pops up is,

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:06.230
pops up is a messaging service.

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:08.840
It's basically a Slack
group for applications.

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:11.510
So it says, OK, I've
found a new file.

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:13.400
That triggers a Cloud Function.

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:17.250
What the Cloud Function does
is it picks up the review

00:29:17.250 --> 00:29:18.930
and it makes the API call.

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:22.805
So it just reads the file and it
sends the plaintext to the API.

00:29:22.805 --> 00:29:25.580
The API runs it through
all the Google magic

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:29.870
and it sends back the fields
that we just walked through.

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:33.170
The Cloud Function waits
for the API to finish.

00:29:33.170 --> 00:29:34.220
It adds that data.

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:37.040
It augments the
file with that data

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:39.740
and puts it back
into cloud storage.

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:42.920
You might wonder why you do the
round trip in cloud storage.

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:46.490
It's because you want to
preserve the initial state so

00:29:46.490 --> 00:29:50.180
that, if you decide to structure
differently in BigQuery

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:51.770
or you want to build
another pipeline,

00:29:51.770 --> 00:29:53.060
you have that data available.

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:54.890
And with the storage
options available,

00:29:54.890 --> 00:29:55.990
it's very competitive.

00:29:55.990 --> 00:29:59.610
It's not expensive, but
you don't have to do that.

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:01.220
You can also
directly [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:03.928
into BigQuery if you want if
that suits your needs better.

00:30:03.928 --> 00:30:05.470
So, from that, it
arrives in BigQuery

00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:07.090
and, from there,
it's structured data

00:30:07.090 --> 00:30:11.950
and it becomes available
for your analytics.

00:30:11.950 --> 00:30:16.830
So I'm going to switch
to the demo right now.

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:19.060
I'm going to show you
what that looks like.

00:30:19.060 --> 00:30:20.010
So I copy pasted--

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:21.840
well, actually,
Sona did, thank you.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:26.950
But she copy pasted the review
that you saw into the website.

00:30:26.950 --> 00:30:29.550
This is a similar online
demo that you can just

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:31.950
use on the Google Cloud
website, and it shows you

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:34.270
how the NLP API works.

00:30:34.270 --> 00:30:37.980
So if we press Analyze,
it does really nice things

00:30:37.980 --> 00:30:40.170
with pretty colors.

00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:42.000
This is the Entities
tab, and it really

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:43.950
shows the different
entities that it

00:30:43.950 --> 00:30:45.790
has identified in this review.

00:30:45.790 --> 00:30:47.820
So it knows its
talking about cavities.

00:30:47.820 --> 00:30:49.800
It knows it's about toothpaste.

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:54.060
It gives a type so it
knows it's consumer goods.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:58.280
It gives you sentiments for
these different elements.

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:00.300
It thinks whitener
is a person, so that

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:03.830
is an interesting observation.

00:31:03.830 --> 00:31:07.410
Colgate [INAUDIBLE] Google
Total is an organization,

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:11.160
and this really opens the
door to also brand tracking.

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:13.650
So you can start
to see at scale how

00:31:13.650 --> 00:31:15.600
people are talking
about your brand,

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:16.920
not just about your product.

00:31:16.920 --> 00:31:19.758
Then you can start to see which
products might invite people

00:31:19.758 --> 00:31:20.800
to talk about your brand.

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:23.820
So where is your brand
exposure the biggest?

00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:27.590
That could inform investment.

00:31:27.590 --> 00:31:28.320
That's not all.

00:31:28.320 --> 00:31:29.260
So we have sentiments.

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:34.010
So we get a score for the
whole entire document,

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:39.060
so that's the 0.7 with a
pretty hefty magnitude.

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:41.850
And then you see what I
promised per sentence.

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:45.213
What is the sentiment and
what is the magnitude.

00:31:45.213 --> 00:31:46.505
Then there is some very fancy--

00:31:49.295 --> 00:31:52.220
if you know languages and
you really want to dig in,

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:54.680
Google also gives
you syntax analysis.

00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:57.050
So it starts to designate
certain words and parts

00:31:57.050 --> 00:31:59.265
of sentences-- what
they mean, the role they

00:31:59.265 --> 00:32:00.140
play in the sentence.

00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:03.240
And this can be
really informative

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:05.810
if you want to start building
models on top of this

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:09.410
if you want to see more
linguistic analysis.

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:09.910
Thank you.

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:13.110
I'm going to switch back
to the presentation.

00:32:13.110 --> 00:32:13.610
All right.

00:32:13.610 --> 00:32:16.280
So I mentioned him three times
now but he's finally coming.

00:32:16.280 --> 00:32:17.530
Tim, thank you for joining us.

00:32:23.177 --> 00:32:24.760
TIM BOOHER: So I'm
a little surprised.

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:25.870
Whitening is a person.

00:32:25.870 --> 00:32:28.060
He runs around in
our technology center

00:32:28.060 --> 00:32:31.810
and puts the secret whitening,
and Google just discovered him.

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:34.990
So that's a trade secret
out there for the world.

00:32:34.990 --> 00:32:36.880
Happy to be here
and have a chance

00:32:36.880 --> 00:32:41.110
to really speak to what we're
doing with Google, which

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:44.290
is super exciting
for me, as I connect

00:32:44.290 --> 00:32:46.690
Colgate's data to
decision makers

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:50.230
to drive growth and value
in our analytics program.

00:32:50.230 --> 00:32:52.810
Key to that is a
flexible set of tools

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:55.610
that empowers us to ride the
wave of technology that's

00:32:55.610 --> 00:32:56.110
there.

00:32:56.110 --> 00:32:57.745
It takes an investment of skill.

00:32:57.745 --> 00:33:00.850
It takes some really
good partnerships

00:33:00.850 --> 00:33:02.150
to be able to do that.

00:33:02.150 --> 00:33:04.960
But once the flywheel
starts moving,

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:07.600
the applications
proliferate really quickly.

00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:09.850
For the demo you
just saw, once we

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:13.060
built that capability we found
around eight different use

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:17.260
cases inside the company
for that corpus of data.

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.810
Much more important
than the data set

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:22.450
is the flexible ability
to go out and gather

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:24.620
the data that we need.

00:33:24.620 --> 00:33:26.560
For example, if we
started on reviews

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:29.380
on an individual
consumer retailer site--

00:33:29.380 --> 00:33:33.910
understood what products people
might be asking for or sizes

00:33:33.910 --> 00:33:37.150
[INAUDIBLE] that we
may not be doing--

00:33:37.150 --> 00:33:39.190
that we might not be
presenting to the world.

00:33:39.190 --> 00:33:41.620
We might then pivot
from that to looking

00:33:41.620 --> 00:33:43.420
at consumer affairs
and our ability

00:33:43.420 --> 00:33:46.210
to respond with the limited
people we have to what people

00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:47.800
are saying about our products.

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:51.550
To translate them into different
applications so that we can

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:54.100
empower the teams
that we already have.

00:33:54.100 --> 00:33:57.640
But data sites like that tend to
be very useful in applications

00:33:57.640 --> 00:34:00.190
like e-commerce optimization.

00:34:00.190 --> 00:34:02.710
What's great about that is we
don't end up with a Balkanized

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:06.820
set of tools, but we're
empowered to take a set of data

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:09.820
and a flexible
acquisition capability,

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:13.000
analysis capability, and
then action capability--

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:16.020
chain those together in a
number of business cases.

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:17.050
And that matters a lot.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:20.560
As a company, we have more than
3 and 1/2 billion customers

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:23.420
that use our products
around the world.

00:34:23.420 --> 00:34:26.739
By and large, the relationships
with those customers

00:34:26.739 --> 00:34:28.659
are inter mediated
by the distributors,

00:34:28.659 --> 00:34:31.719
by the retailers that we
sell those products through.

00:34:31.719 --> 00:34:34.540
So understanding
what consumers want,

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:36.489
what they think
about our products--

00:34:36.489 --> 00:34:39.550
it's really important to be
able to use the power tools

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:41.150
that you're shown here.

00:34:41.150 --> 00:34:44.199
And it's important to put it in
the context that Sjef showed,

00:34:44.199 --> 00:34:46.480
where we're able to move
around blue hexagons

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:49.330
and quickly rearrange and then
agilely focus our business

00:34:49.330 --> 00:34:51.130
where the value is going to be.

00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:54.550
The three points made here
are first driven by the fact

00:34:54.550 --> 00:34:58.750
that the complex ecosystem
that we're at in the world

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:03.370
is discernible by the tools
that you've seen here.

00:35:03.370 --> 00:35:06.280
That takes engineering
and writing code.

00:35:06.280 --> 00:35:08.590
It takes an
investment to partner

00:35:08.590 --> 00:35:10.330
and build that internally.

00:35:10.330 --> 00:35:12.730
That's a major effort of ours.

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:15.400
The second part is we
need to have partnerships

00:35:15.400 --> 00:35:18.370
to prime the pump to show
what's possible in this type

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:19.610
of context.

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:21.820
Once you do that you're
past a barrier of entry

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:24.620
and you can start to
present to business users,

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:27.040
whether it's in marketing,
supply chain, product

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:30.400
development, and start to
proliferate the use cases.

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:32.320
And then, you enter
into a virtuous cycle

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:35.740
where demand for this type
of capability goes up,

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:38.290
and you're able to increase
your resource investment

00:35:38.290 --> 00:35:40.670
and capability in this area.

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:42.610
And I think it's pretty
clear from everyone

00:35:42.610 --> 00:35:46.030
that using GCP for
us has been really

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:48.430
helpful to start with
a low barrier of entry

00:35:48.430 --> 00:35:51.670
and quickly move into
doing complex things

00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:55.720
that we're able to expand
across a diverse base of users.

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:58.140
I would love to talk to
you afterwards about this,

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:00.702
and thanks to Sjef for
setting this up for us

00:36:00.702 --> 00:36:01.660
and showing us the way.

00:36:09.065 --> 00:36:09.940
SONA OAKLEY: Awesome.

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:12.970
So we will go ahead and
do a really quick wrap up

00:36:12.970 --> 00:36:15.710
of what we've seen here today.

00:36:15.710 --> 00:36:17.920
So essentially, you saw
two different examples

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:19.780
of how we can start
to get insights

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:21.970
from unstructured data at scale.

00:36:21.970 --> 00:36:24.970
The problems that both Colgate
and Jellyfish articulated

00:36:24.970 --> 00:36:26.320
are not new problems.

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:29.080
They are things that we've
been dealing with and finding

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:30.280
workarounds for.

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.530
What's really new here
is the innovations

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:35.020
that Google Cloud has been
able to bring from a tools

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:37.690
and infrastructure
perspective to really help

00:36:37.690 --> 00:36:40.100
solve these problems at scale.

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:43.480
And that is, to Tim's point
and to James' point, the power

00:36:43.480 --> 00:36:46.690
